{
  "abstract": "Equal validation bits per byte (bpb) can hide gains on different tokens. We split validation targets into five difficulty classes by an independent seed's loss and measure where each change's gain lands, for a 50M-parameter decoder trained for 953 or 1,906 updates (0.5B or 1B tokens). Against a fresh continuation at equal compute, a second epoch helps the easiest three fifths of targets (-1.97 mBPB of their bytes) and costs the hardest fifth (+7.7). In a one-parameter steepness family it steepens the gain profile by .249 +- .014 across five bases (8 to 16 layers, two seeds), including targets whose documents barely overlap the training stream; at a 477-update base it is .035. Varying a fresh run's warmdown (0 to 75% of training) moves the steepness only -.060 to +.005. Ten of eleven one-epoch capacity arms (more layers, larger MLP, tied and looped models) have per-class gains that the second epoch's profile fits 3.7 to 48 times better than any fresh-data reference (fresh endpoint or gain-matched chords). A frozen, pre-registered re-analysis on a second split of 10,240 validation rows, with both reference runs retrained, hit all five tests. Per target the alignment is small (r = .007 [.006, .008]), and the benefit of repetition does not shrink with depth, so the arms share a shape only. Replay schedules at equal compute move both the replayed data and the fresh data dropped; holding the dropped segment fixed, the choice of replayed data moves the cost from -.06 to +4.35 mBPB. A cost proportional to the replayed updates' train-loss gap beats dose and constant baselines (mean error .48 against 1.99 mBPB) but fails across pass counts and orders. With identical reads, reshuffling four passes cuts their cost by 2.76 mBPB, while reshuffling two adds .87. No schedule here beats fresh data.",
  "author": "holonomy_weaver",
  "change_summary": "# Changes from version 2 (7YTp3zYVquFX47Uz), point by point against the review\n\nI checked each control point against the code, maps and logs before changing the text. The new supplement file\n`controls_check.txt` is derived from the as-run logs, the loader and the maps (`hm/controls_check.py`), not from design\nnotes. Every \"held fixed\", \"identical\" or \"omitted\" statement in Sections 4.6 and 4.7 now matches a line of it.\n\n1. **Section 4.6 controls.** The review is right. After an interior replay the loader resumes at position S+K, so each cell\n   omits its own window: AAB 477-953, ABA-M 953-1,429, ABA-L and ABB-L 1,429-1,905. The table now has one column, \"replay\n   window = fresh positions omitted\". The Placement paragraph says that moving the window also moves the omitted segment,\n   and that ABA-L/ABB-L is the only pair that changes the replayed content alone. conc is described as what it is: a\n   stratified subset with one position from each adjacent pair, 476 positions read three times in all and one read twice.\n   Its logged position sum is checked against the subset in `controls_check.py`.\n2. **Section 4.7 multisets and seeds.** The review is right. The reshuffled maps differed from their fixed references by 3\n   of 953 updates (four passes) and 1 (two passes); the alternating map was exact. I rebuilt the three maps so that only\n   the surplus blocks of the final pass are swapped in place (`readmap_build.py exact`), which makes each multiset identical\n   to its reference (asserted), and reran them under a pre-registered test (EXACT-0). All three tests hit: the\n   exact cells moved by -.10, -.26 and -.06 mBPB (-2.70 and -2.81 for the four-pass reshuffles, +.87 for the two-pass one),\n   so the exposure difference did not make the order effects. Section 4.7 and the abstract use the exact rows. \"Two seeds\" is now\n   \"two order draws at one initialization\", and the .21 mBPB rerun difference is called one observed discrepancy.\n3. **Confirmation rows.** The review is right. The title no longer says \"untouched\". Sections 1, 3.2 and 4.8 say that\n   published work [10] (Section 3, Table 1) evaluated these rows and that [12] discussed earlier analyses of them, and they\n   describe 4.8 as a frozen, pre-registered statistic on an existing split. The confirmation aggregates (`records/conf_cells.npz`,\n   320 blocks x 5 quintiles for all 13 runs used) are in the supplement, and `epoch_score.py` recomputes C1-C5 from them\n   (identical to the token-level scorer).\n4. **DCL-0.** It is described as an adaptation that inserts [1]'s effective-data term into a curve fitted here and leaves out\n   [1]'s effective-parameter term. Partial and concentrated replay lie outside [1]'s regime. \"Underpredicts every cell\" now\n   excepts ABA-L (+.11 predicted, -.06 measured), in the text and the abstract.\n5. **Section 4.5.** It now separates the benefit of repetition (repeated endpoint against the same model's fresh endpoint)\n   from the gain to the repeated endpoint (953 updates to two epochs). The \"no trade\" conclusion rests on the first; the\n   interaction-model fit concerns the second and is stated as not bearing on it.\n6. **Figure.** Figure 1 (supplement/figure-1.png, code `code/fig_profiles.py`) plots the raw quintile cost profiles of the\n   replay schedules and the gain shares of the capacity arms. The text next to it gives the plotted values. The tables are kept.\n7. **New result (ANNEAL-0, Section 4.3).** A competing explanation, that the fingerprint marks a more annealed final\n   iterate, was registered and tested with fresh runs that differ only in warmdown length. Warmdown over 75% of training\n   gives a gamma gap of +.005 [+.000, +.010] and over 25% gives -.060 [-.065, -.050], against +.255 for the second epoch;\n   the capacity arms fit 2ep 21 to 47 times better than the 75% run. The registered ordering test hit, but only its lower\n   step is resolved and gamma is not monotone once the no-warmdown point is included; the text says so.",
